    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 28 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 28. Navigating the special education system can be daunting. This directory provides key contacts, but remember that effective advocacy involves understanding your rights and responsibilities under both federal and New York State law.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 3 (Serving District 28)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>90-27 Sutphin Blvd, Jamaica, NY 11435</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-557-2553">718-557-2553</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
                <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #777; margin-top: 5px;"><i>CSE 3 is responsible for students with IEPs attending schools within their zone of District 28.</i></p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep meticulous records of all communication attempts, including dates, times, methods of contact (phone, email), and the names of individuals you contacted.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(i)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> (or "Request for Independent Educational Evaluation - [Student Name]", etc. depending on the request).</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names. A simple spreadsheet is ideal. Include date, time, method of communication, person contacted, and a brief summary of the conversation.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444; margin-top: 10px;">
                This is crucial for establishing a clear record of your communications and ensuring compliance with legal timelines. Remember, the burden of proof often falls on the parent to demonstrate that the district was properly notified.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal). This adds another layer of accountability.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Research the specific name and email address for District 28.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or the district fails to meet its obligations. You can find the complaint form on the NYSED website. This formally documents the district's non-compliance and triggers a formal investigation.</li>
            </ol>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444; margin-top: 10px;">
                <strong>Important Note:</strong> Before filing a State Complaint, consider requesting Mediation. Mediation is a less adversarial process where a neutral third party helps you and the district reach an agreement. While not legally binding unless formalized in a settlement agreement, it can often resolve disputes more quickly and amicably than a formal complaint.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What is the difference between the CSE and CPSE?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            The <strong>Committee on Special Education (CSE)</strong> is responsible for students aged 5-21 who are suspected of having or have been identified as having a disability and require special education services. The <strong>Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE)</strong> serves children aged 3-5 with suspected disabilities. This distinction is governed by NYS Education Law Article 89 and Part 200 Regulations.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What if I disagree with the school's evaluation of my child?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            You have the right to obtain an <strong>Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE)</strong> at public expense if you disagree with the school's evaluation (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)). You must request the IEE <em>in writing</em>. The district then has the option to either fund the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to defend its evaluation. The IEE results must be considered by the CSE.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Advocacy Tip:</strong> Request a list of approved IEE providers from the district. If you choose a provider not on the list, be prepared to justify your choice, as the district may be less likely to fund it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What is an IEP and how is it developed?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            An <strong>Individualized Education Program (IEP)</strong> is a written plan that outlines the special education services and supports a student with a disability needs to succeed in school. It is developed by the CSE, which includes parents, teachers, school psychologists, and other relevant professionals. The IEP must be based on the student's individual needs and must be designed to provide the student with a Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) in the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE), as mandated by the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Advocacy Tip:</strong> Prepare for the IEP meeting by reviewing your child's records, making a list of your concerns, and developing specific goals you want the IEP to address. Bring any outside evaluations or reports to the meeting.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">My child's IEP isn't being implemented. What can I do?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
            First, document every instance of non-implementation, including dates, times, specific services missed, and the impact on your child. Then, contact the CSE chairperson <em>in writing</em> to request a meeting to address the issue. If the problem persists, you may need to file a State Complaint with NYSED or request mediation or a due process hearing. Failure to implement an IEP is a violation of IDEA.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Common Pitfall:</strong> Districts sometimes claim a lack of resources as a reason for non-implementation. This is generally not a valid excuse. The district is legally obligated to provide the services outlined in the IEP, regardless of budgetary constraints.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What are my rights during CSE meetings?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">
           You have the right to actively participate in all CSE meetings, present information, ask questions, and disagree with the committee's recommendations. You have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to the meeting. The CSE must consider your input and make decisions based on your child's individual needs. You also have the right to receive copies of all documents considered by the CSE.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="resources-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-book-open"></i> Additional Resources
    </h2>

    <ul style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>New York State Education Department (NYSED) Special Education Website:</strong> <a href="http://www.nysed.gov/specialed/" target="_blank">www.nysed.gov/specialed/</a> - Provides comprehensive information on special education laws, regulations, and policies in New York State.</li>
        <li><strong>Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA):</strong> <a href="https://sites.ed.gov/idea/" target="_blank">https://sites.ed.gov/idea/</a> - The federal law that governs special education in the United States.</li>
        <li><strong>NYC Department of Education Special Education:</strong> Search the DOE website for their special education resources.</li>
        <li><strong>Disability Rights New York (DRNY):</strong> <a href="https://www.drny.org/" target="_blank">https://www.drny.org/</a> - A non-profit organization that provides legal assistance and advocacy services to people with disabilities in New York State.</li>
        <li><strong>INCLUDEnyc:</strong> <a href="https://www.includenyc.org/" target="_blank">https://www.includenyc.org/</a> - A New York City-based organization that provides information, training, and advocacy services to families of children with disabilities.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

